// Courtroom Drama: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== courtroom-drama.f01 | feature | three-act
title: A Suit For Freedom
genre: courtroom-drama + legal-drama
tone: Contained, formal and burning underneath
era: 1844 | period
setting: The Circuit Court, St. Louis, Missouri | real | court
logline: An enslaved laundress sues her owner for her freedom under a Missouri statute, and must prove in open court, before a jury of slaveholders, that she once spent four months on free soil.
premise: The law allows it: a person held in slavery may sue as a poor person if they can show they were taken to live where slavery is forbidden. {lucy} was hired out to an army officer in the Illinois country as a girl. Nobody who saw her there is both alive and willing. While the suit runs, the court hires her out and holds her wages.
synopsis: {lucy} learns of the statute from the women she washes with; three of them have filed. Her lawyer, {thackeray}, takes such cases because they pay from the county and because he likes to win. The first act is the filing: her owner, {mrs_berthold}, is astonished and wounded, and the sheriff leases {lucy} to a steamboat laundry for the length of the case. The second act is the search for a witness. The officer is dead. The post's sutler remembers her but will not come. {polly}, a free Black woman who runs a boarding house and has won her own suit, finds a discharged private who was on the same post, a drunk whom a jury will despise. The defence argues {lucy} was only passing through, a sojourn and not a residence; four months becomes a question of how long a pot must sit on a fire before it is a household. {thackeray} wants to rest on the law. {lucy} wants to speak, and by law she cannot testify against a white person. The third act is the trial. The private is torn apart on the stand. Then {thackeray} calls the one witness nobody expected: {mrs_berthold}, who under oath, to her own horror, cannot lie about a letter she wrote that winter complaining that her girl was "settled up north." The jury is out nine minutes. {lucy} walks out with papers and with wages for the two years the suit took, less the sheriff's fee. Her daughter, born after the Illinois winter, is still the property of the estate. {lucy} asks {thackeray} what it costs to file again.
question: What is a right worth when you must prove it to the people it was taken by?
themes: law as a weapon with a handle on both ends; who is allowed to speak; freedom by increments
audience: Adults; students
viewpoint: With {lucy}, who may watch her own trial and not speak in it
stakes: Her freedom, two years' wages, sale down the river if she loses, and her daughter either way
conflict: {lucy}'s plain fact against a court that will not hear it from her own mouth
ending: She wins on her owner's unwilling testimony, and files again the same day for her child.
experience: Dread sharpened by procedure, one gasp in a courtroom, and a victory that immediately asks for another
look: River light, whitewashed courtroom, steam and lye; {lucy} always framed behind a rail
sound: A gavel, steamboat whistles, wet linen. The clerk reading names.
m: prove | system | justice-vs-mercy | bittersweet
@ lucy | Lucy Tolliver | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 31 | Her freedom, on paper | She knows exactly where she stood that winter | She is not permitted to say so in court | Patient to the point of menace | From a claimant who may not speak to one who files for someone else | Low, exact, wastes nothing | Starched apron, hands cracked by lye
@ thackeray | Amos Thackeray | supporting | ally | human | he/him | 37 | To win, on the statute | These suits are the only ones where the law is plainly on his side | He thinks the law is enough | Clever, cool, less brave than his client | From resting on the law to risking a hostile witness | Dry Vermont precision | Black coat shiny at the elbows
@ mrs_berthold | Emilie Chouinard | supporting | antagonist | human | she/her | 55 | To keep what she was left | She believes she has been kind | A Bible oath she will not break | Pious, proprietary, honest against her interest | From wounded owner to the witness who frees her | French-inflected, formal, hurt | Widow's black, a crucifix she touches before answering
@ polly | Polly Greer | supporting | mentor | human | she/her | 48 | To see the next woman through | She won her own suit after five years | Witnesses are for sale and she has no money | Tough, connected, unsurprised by anything | From advising to sitting in the gallery every day | Fast, practical, laughs at lawyers | Good bonnet, boarding-house keys
~ lucy - thackeray | client and counsel | a case he likes | a client he obeys
~ lucy - mrs_berthold | enslaved woman and her owner | "after all I have done" | an oath that costs one of them everything
~ lucy - polly | claimant and the woman who went before | a name passed over a wash tub | a second petition, written out together
# The Petition | EXT | Levee Wash-House | DAY | lucy, polly | Hear of the law | Over the tubs, {polly} says what the statute needs: a place, a time, a witness | {lucy} decides to file | 4 | water, crowd=10, props=wash tubs
# The Petition | INT | Thackeray's Office | DAY | lucy, thackeray | Tell it once | She gives him the winter exactly: the post, the officer, the months. He writes "four" and underlines it | | 4 | props=petition+pen
# The Petition | INT | Chouinard House, Parlour | DAY | lucy, mrs_berthold | Be served | The sheriff's paper arrives. {mrs_berthold} asks what she has ever done to deserve it | The court hires {lucy} out | 4
# The Witness | INT | Polly's Boarding House | NIGHT | lucy, polly, thackeray | Settle for a drunk | The only man who will swear to Illinois cannot sit straight. {thackeray} would rather rest on the law | | 5 | props=bottle
# The Trial | INT | Circuit Court | DAY | lucy, thackeray, mrs_berthold, polly | Lose the witness | The private is destroyed on cross. "A sojourn," the defence says. {lucy} half rises and is told to sit | The case is nearly lost | 6 | crowd=60
# The Trial | INT | Circuit Court | DAY | lucy, thackeray, mrs_berthold, polly | Call her owner | {thackeray} hands {mrs_berthold} her own letter. She touches the crucifix. "Settled," she reads. "I wrote settled." | Her owner's oath frees her | 7 | crowd=60, props=letter
# The Trial | EXT | Courthouse Steps | DUSK | lucy, thackeray, polly | File again | Papers in hand, wages less the sheriff's fee. {lucy} asks what a second petition costs | | 3 | props=freedom papers
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from the St. Louis freedom suits, with invented people
